parts.body:
Medigap policies are **[[standardized-medigap-plans|standardized]]**: in most states there are 10 lettered plans (A–D, F, G and K–N). Policies with the same letter have **the same benefits** no matter which company sells them — **price is the only difference**.

**[[medigap-core-benefits|Core (basic) benefits]]** are in every plan (Plan A has only these; Plans K and L pay some at a percentage):

- Part A hospital coinsurance, plus coverage for an additional **365 days** of hospital care after Medicare hospital benefits are used up;
- Part B coinsurance or copayment;
- the first **3 pints of blood** each year;
- Part A hospice coinsurance or copayment.

**Additional benefits** found in some other plans include the Part A deductible, skilled nursing facility coinsurance, the Part B deductible, Part B excess charges and foreign travel emergency care.

In New York, every application must include the right to apply for **Plans A and B and either D or G**. **Plans C and F** may be sold only to people who first became eligible for Medicare **before January 1, 2020**.

Every Medigap issuer, directly or through its producers, must (11 NYCRR 58.1(g)(1)):

- make sure any **comparison of policies is fair and accurate**;
- make sure **excessive insurance** is not sold;
- display on the first page: **'Notice to buyer: This policy may not cover all of your medical expenses.'**;
- make every reasonable effort to find out whether the applicant **already has accident and health insurance**, and what kind and how much;
- keep auditable compliance procedures.

In addition to the Insurance Law's general prohibitions, three practices are specifically prohibited (58.1(g)(2)):

- **[[twisting|Twisting]]** — knowingly making a misleading representation or incomplete or fraudulent comparison to get someone to drop, surrender or replace a policy;
- **High-pressure tactics** — inducing a purchase through force, fright, threat or undue pressure;
- **[[cold-lead-advertising|Cold lead advertising]]** — marketing that fails to conspicuously disclose that it is an insurance solicitation and that an agent or insurer will make contact.
